Contact material callback

Adds b3ContactMaterial, b3ContactMaterialFcn and
b3World_SetContactMaterialCallback( worldId, fcn, context ). When a callback is
set, every touching contact the narrow phase updates calls it after its
manifold is computed and before the solver reads it, with the two shape ids,
the friction, restitution, rolling resistance and tangent velocity mixed from
the shapes' surface materials, and the context. The callback may change any of
them; the contact keeps what it writes until its next update. Convex, compound,
mesh and height-field contacts all call it. A contact the pre-solve callback
disables does not.

Contact recycling skips the narrow phase, and with it the callback, for a
contact whose bodies barely moved, and a resting contact is recycled on every
step. So setting the callback clears the recycling state of every contact, and
b3World_InvalidateContactMaterials( worldId ) and
b3Shape_InvalidateContactMaterials( shapeId ) do the same for a world or one
shape when the callback's answers change. They wake nothing: a sleeping
contact updates when its island wakes. Both are recorded, as local ops 0x7E
and 0x7D, and B3_REC_LOCAL_EXTENSIONS moves to level 2; the callback itself is
not recorded, like the pre-solve callback.

The callback runs on the worker threads of the step and writes only to the
contact being updated, so the result does not depend on the thread count or
the order of the updates. A negative or non-finite friction, restitution or
rolling resistance, or a non-finite tangent velocity, keeps the mixed value.
Without a callback, and without an invalidation, the narrow phase is
unchanged.

